        "original_id": "a0299821-f246-58fe-a181-059733253677",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Colin Chapman, Maurice Philippe\n- Products: Lotus 49, Lotus 19, Cosworth DFV\n- Companies: Ford, Lotus Cars\n- Works: 9 Days in Summer\n- Events: 1967 Formula One season\n\nFacts:\n- Colin Chapman was born on 19 May 1928 and died on 16 December 1982.\n- Colin Chapman was an influential English design engineer, inventor, and builder in the automotive industry, and he founded Lotus Cars.\n- The Lotus 19, also called the Monte Carlo, was a mid-engine sports-racing car designed by Colin Chapman of Lotus and built from 1960 until 1962.\n- The Lotus 49 was a Formula One racing car designed by Colin Chapman and Maurice Philippe for the 1967 Formula One season.\n- In 1967, the Lotus 49 took part in nine Formula One races.\n- Ford made the promotional documentary film 9 Days in Summer.\n- 9 Days in Summer tells the story of the development of the Ford-funded Cosworth DFV and the Lotus 49, and its title refers to the nine Formula One races contested by the Lotus 49 in 1967.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b67dfcf9-6e69-569f-b1bc-e2a116ae7a5b",
        "text": "Page: Lotus 49. Sentence: The Lotus 49 was a Formula One racing car designed by Colin Chapman and Maurice Philippe for the 1967 F1 season.\nPage: Colin Chapman. Sentence: Anthony Colin Bruce Chapman CBE (19 May 1928 – 16 December 1982) was an influential English design engineer, inventor, and builder in the automotive industry, and founder of Lotus Cars.\nPage: 9 Days in Summer. Sentence: 9 Days in Summer is a promotional documentary film made by Ford that tells the story of the development of the Ford-funded Cosworth DFV and Lotus 49, the title coming from the nine Formula One races the car took part in 1967, which are also extensively featured in the film.\nPage: Lotus 19. Sentence: The Lotus 19 or Monte Carlo is a mid-engine sports-racing car designed by Colin Chapman of Lotus and built from 1960 until 1962.",
